CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Second drafts should be submitted by November 28 (5pm). \n\nOR
 IGINAL ASSIGNMENT\n\nFollowing on from Daniel's class on Thursday\, you ge
 t to create a draft of your résumé. The assignment will be available in 
 Quercus (CSC2701H) from September 3 and is due by September 27\, 2024.
  \n\n \n\nPlease submit a one page résumé to us in Word format
 .  This makes it easier for us to edit/make comments on. (If you are a
  LaTeX user\, please submit a PDF.)\n\nA good starting point for your r
 ésumés may be the version you already have\, or you can start with a fre
 sh copy if you like. An editable template and two examples are included:
 \n\n* [Résumé Example 1] (https://q.utoronto.ca/courses/395436/files/387
 81379?verifier=gKP4HYB5vhE9NV5pX9Ghn12bosMftIdMCk4Tf3eo&wrap=1)\n\n* [Rés
 umé Example 2] (https://q.utoronto.ca/courses/395436/files/38781375?verif
 ier=Nij5sVDXLwx7orXwE66d7WkphIzAJi3NIfSGDUPy&wrap=1)\n\n* [MScAC Resume Te
 mplate] (https://q.utoronto.ca/courses/395436/files/38781387?verifier=OiNu
 d5XPsq33PtIAF2gAt4Ut0jNcaaxLlBf9AW1i&wrap=1) (Word format)\n\nThe résumé
  you send will be considered an initial draft — it will not go to indust
 ry partners. We will be editing spelling\, punctuation\, grammar\, and for
 matting. Your résumé will also be looked over for technical content. It 
 is hoped that\, via two or three iterations\, we will have a close to perf
 ect set of résumés by January 6. These will be shown off to industry par
 tners.\n\nBelow are some tips and general advice that should be noted befo
 re you begin to create your résumés — please read these carefully. The
 se were provided by some of the hiring managers amongst the alums.\n\nAdvi
 ce on small details\n-----------------------\n\n* Use appropriate file nam
 ing conventions — the "Surname_Name_VersionX.docx” file name conventio
 n is preferred.  For example: 'Mosses_Claire_Version1.docx' \n\n* Ensur
 e all links to e-mail addresses/personal pages are clickable.  If you ar
 e linking to a personal webpage/Linkedin profile/GitHub repo also ensure t
 hat it is up-to-date.\n\n* Ensure that all the information (even the sma
 ll information) detailed in your résumé is specific and accurately f
 ormatted\, for example\, 'mysql' should be 'MySQL'\n\n* Keep the résumé 
 to one page.\n\n* Use bullet points.\n\n* Use correct spelling\, punctuati
 on and grammar.\n\nGeneral advice on content\n-------------------------\n\
 n* Order your sections appropriately\, and use the appropriate sections (n
 ote the annotation of optional beside certain sections of the template). Y
 ou don't have to use them all.\n\n* Include the names of important courses
 . Industry partners are interested in the graduate training you have\, but
  you may also have additional relevant training from other degrees. UofT c
 ourse numbers often don't mean anything to people external to the UofT.\n\
 n* Ensure that the most impressive items are close to the top. For example
 \, if you have a lot of work experience\, the sections you use may be: Exp
 erience/Education/Skills/Awards.  If you have little/no work experienc
 e you may have Skills/Education/Other Experience/Awards. You do not have 
 to use all sections on the template.\n\n* Get creative — have a play aro
 und with font/colour schemes and design — a few résumés last year were
  created in LaTeX\, which looked very professional.\n\nFinal tips\n-------
 ---\n\n* Try and avoid using commas instead of the word "and" — even if 
 it saves space\, this isn't correct.\n\n* If you're going to use links to 
 github pages make sure they have substantial content on them. You don't 
 have to include them\, so if whatever is behind the links is largely inact
 ive\, then you can take them out.  \n\n*\n\nListing "Microsoft Office" 
 as a skill in a graduate-level computer science resume is only acceptable 
 if you've done advanced things\, such as building VBA macros for Excel. 
  Otherwise\, it's expected that you will have this ability and you do not 
 need to state it.  \n\n* Try to avoid using phrases such as "I used tec
 hnologies like ..." and "I worked with Hadoop\, NiFi etc..." This is suppo
 sed to be a professional document\, so it's preferable to steer away from 
 using 'like' and ‘etc’ in this manner.\n\n* Where possible\, try and m
 ention the technologies used. "I built a platform for ..." is a lot less m
 eaningful than "I built a platform using Java and Spring for..."\n\n* Avoi
 d name-dropping professors and managers. These may be more appropriate t
 o mention when applying for research positions\, as opposed to industry po
 sitions. For the most part\, it will likely be assumed that supervisors ar
 e legit. Additionally\, it may be unlikely that your professors are kn
 own to the company (unless they are household names).
SEQUENCE:0
